{
  "requirement": "The book preprocessor MUST escape requirement names and link targets when inserting badge HTML. It MUST encode badge label and message text in Shields.io URL path components so punctuation cannot change the badge URL structure.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The tests adequately cover `requirements.references.safe_badges`. I read the implementation only to see which paths insert badge HTML and what encoding they use. I derived the expected behavior from the requirement.\n\n**Obligations**\n1. Requirement names and link targets MUST be HTML-escaped when badge HTML is inserted.\n2. Badge label and message text MUST be encoded in Shields.io URL path components, so punctuation cannot change the URL structure.\n\n**Obligation 2: path-component encoding**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[badge_urls_percent_encode_punctuation_and_html_is_escaped]` calls `shield_badge(\"a/b?c\", \"x#y%z&w<v>\\\"u' café\", …)`. It asserts the exact full `src` URL.\n  - `/ ? # % & < > \" '` and the non-ASCII `é` come out percent-encoded (`é` as `%C3%A9`).\n  - A space becomes `_`.\n  - The `&` before `labelColor` is written as `&amp;`.\n  - The `alt` text is HTML-escaped.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[shields_badge_urls_escape_requirement_names]` covers the Shields-specific escapes `_` to `__` and `-` to `--`. It also covers `.` passing through unchanged and a space becoming `_` in `Determination false`.\n- Together they exercise every branch of `shield_path_component` (unreserved characters, space, `_`, `-`, and percent-encoding for everything else). The encoder is a default-percent-encode allow-list, so other punctuation is not a distinct input class.\n\n**Obligation 1: escaping names and link targets**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[definition_evidence_link_targets_are_escaped]` uses a detail-page path containing `& \" < >`. It asserts the escaped `href` on the name badge and the `title`. It also asserts that the raw `a&b`, `\"q\"` and `<x>` do not appear.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[reference_link_targets_are_escaped]` uses a target chapter path containing the same special characters. It asserts that the `{cite}` badge `href` (built in `reference_badge`) is escaped and that no raw `\"q\"` or `<x>` leaks.\n- The `escaped_name` handling in `replace_definitions` is the one piece not driven with special characters. Its own comment says valid names cannot contain HTML-special characters, so it cannot be driven end to end. It is the same `escape_html` transformation that the tests above check, so this is not a gap.\n\n**Other paths and scope**\n- Every shield badge goes through `shield_badge`. That includes the reference, query-directive, and index/detail evidence badges, so the exact-URL tests cover them.\n- The term-link `href` in `replace_inline_references` is not a badge. It is outside this requirement's text.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept. The marked tests assert specific escaped outputs, not just execution.",
